From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0300-task-and-mesh-programs-as-compute-shaders.patch

Source: 0300-task-and-mesh-programs-as-compute-shaders.patch
Off by default: RuntimeInfo::mesh_as_compute (set by the Vulkan backend for
EDEN_NX2_MESH_AS_COMPUTE=1, patch 0301). With it a task or mesh program is emitted
as GLCompute instead of TaskEXT/MeshEXT, and the mesh epilogue writes the stage
buffer's lines to a scratch region instead of mesh outputs: a draw header
(VkDrawIndirectCommand), the task counts and payloads, a (task, index) table per
mesh group, absolute vertex indices per primitive (a group's vertex 0 three times
for a primitive the program did not emit: a degenerate triangle) and the vertex and
primitive records. EmitMeshReadbackVertex generates the vertex shader that reads
them back for a plain triangle-list draw; mesh_compute_scan.comp (SPIR-V 1.3,
embedded as mesh_compute_scan_spv.h) turns the task counts into the table and the
draw and dispatch headers. The region is reached through physical storage buffer
addresses from push constants after the graphics ones (mesh_compute_layout.h: the
address as two 32-bit words, since a 64-bit member read as zero on one driver).

After AnyPS5 PR 9356 (boykopovar, f49a25a): the same compute-then-readback design;
the records here are the NVN stage buffer's 32-bit lines, and the task stage,
which that port has no equivalent of, gets a scan dispatch in between.

Checked on the Mac: the core's own emit path (a hand-built task and mesh IR
program) gives modules spirv-val accepts for Vulkan 1.3; the task, scan and mesh
passes run on an Apple M1 Max through MoltenVK 1.2.10 write the scratch region
exactly as a Python model of the layout predicts, for a lone mesh draw and for 1
and 3 task workgroups with the group cap in force. The readback shader is
validated but not run. Nothing was built for or run on the console.

Known limits: per-primitive PrimitiveId is the rasterizer's, not the program's;
point and line topologies are refused earlier; an unmapped payload word a mesh
program reads holds whatever the task's workgroup memory held, as on the hardware.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
